      "hints": [
        "Integrate $f''(x) = (4x + 1)^{-1/2}$ to get $f'(x) = \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4x + 1} + c$.",
        "Use minimum at $x = 2 \\implies f'(2) = 0 \\implies c = -\\frac{3}{2}$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Integrate the second derivative $f''(x) = (4x + 1)^{-\\frac{1}{2}}$ with respect to $x$:\n$$f'(x) = \\int (4x + 1)^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\,dx = \\frac{(4x + 1)^{\\frac{1}{2}}}{\\frac{1}{2} \\times 4} + c = \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4x + 1} + c$$",
        "Since $f$ has a stationary minimum at $x = 2$, $f'(2) = 0$:\n$$f'(2) = \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4(2) + 1} + c = 0 \\implies \\frac{1}{2}(3) + c = 0 \\implies c = -\\frac{3}{2}$$\n$$f'(x) = \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4x + 1} - \\frac{3}{2}$$"
      ],
